    <string name="reset_title">Reset chroot</string>
    <string name="reset_body">Deletes /data/local/arch completely, including all files inside the chroot.</string>
    <string name="reset_confirm_body">This cannot be undone. Delete the entire Arch Linux installation?</string>
    <string name="reset_button">Delete everything</string>
    <string name="cancel">Cancel</string>

    <string name="about_body">Arch Linux ARM (aarch64) running in a real chroot at %1$s. The chroot survives app uninstalls; only \"Reset chroot\" removes it.</string>
    <string name="about_limits_title">Known limitations</string>
    <string name="about_limits_body">No systemd inside a chroot (use plain scripts for services). Kernels older than 4.14 may break newer glibc calls. Kernels built without CONFIG_SYSVIPC cannot run databases like PostgreSQL.</string>
    <string name="about_credits_title">Credits</string>
    <string name="reset_confirm_final_title">Last chance</string>
    <string name="reset_confirm_final_body">The whole rootfs at /data/local/arch is about to be deleted. Installed packages, your home directory and anything you saved inside Arch are gone for good.</string>
    <string name="reset_confirm_final_button">Delete everything</string>
    <string name="mounts_busy_title">Mounts still active</string>
    <string name="mounts_busy_body">Nothing was deleted. These mount points are still live under the chroot — a delete would reach through them into the real filesystem. Unmount from Home and try again.</string>
    <string name="mounts_active_title">Active right now</string>
    <string name="mounts_active_empty">Nothing mounted</string>
    <string name="mounts_refresh">Refresh</string>
    <string name="mount_failed">Mount failed</string>
    <string name="terminal_broken_title">The chroot is incomplete</string>
    <string name="terminal_broken_body">These files are missing, so there is nothing to log into. Reinstall Arch from the wizard.</string>
    <string name="mounts_live_title">Chroot mounted</string>
    <string name="mounts_live_body">While these are mounted, do not delete %1$s by hand. A delete would follow them out of the chroot and into the real filesystem they point at.</string>
    <string name="mounts_dev_mode_title">How /dev is provided</string>
    <string name="mounts_dev_use_android">Use Android\'s /dev</string>
    <string name="mounts_dev_private_hint">A private /dev is mounted with only the nodes a shell needs (null, zero, random, urandom, tty, ptmx). Nothing inside the chroot folder leads to the phone\'s real device nodes.</string>
    <string name="mounts_dev_bind_hint">Android\'s /dev is bind-mounted in. Maximum compatibility, but while it is mounted a delete of the chroot folder reaches the phone\'s real device nodes and can bring the system down.</string>
